landing-review: vulkan-only plan, landing 10c-3 -- the live-state log moves to the game thread

Range reviewed: main...HEAD (0470c2b..76077e4, 3 commits).
Reviewer: dedicated read-only agent, `high` (CLAUDE.md: "changes the
synchronisation between the game thread and the render thread" -- this
moves the roster/header/mouse logging off the render thread and onto the
game thread's publish path).
TWO rounds. Round 1 returned findings that were fixed with new code;
round 2 returned NO HIGH, confirmed all four of round 1's fixes, and
called the s_lastFilled stamp "correct and provably complete".

WHAT LANDED. log_units left tagpu_overlay.c -- the render thread, reached
only by render_ogl.c and render_vk.c, so on renderer=gdi it never ran and
`tacli roster` could not answer. It is now roster_log() in
tagpu_packet_pub.c, called from the game thread off the packet that was
just filled, behind three independent QPC gates: ROSTER_HDR_MS 500 for
`units:`, ROSTER_DUMP_MS 5000 for the unit block, ROSTER_MOUSE_MS 250 for
`mouse:`. A keepalive forces a publish when no fill has happened for
ROSTER_HDR_MS, because on a lane with no consumer the FRESH gate skips
every unforced publish and fill_frame would otherwise run about once per
level -- the gdi starvation trap.

ROUND 1's FINDINGS, ALL FIXED IN CODE.
 - The force asked the MOUSE cadence (the shortest), which published
   twice as often as anything needs. It asks ROSTER_HDR_MS now: the
   cadence of the slowest line that is actually read.
 - `mouse:` was the verification argument and nothing parses it. tacli's
   structured readers are PEEK_RX, ROSTER_RX and SCN_LIVE_RX; the only
   other mention of `mouse: screen=` in the tree is prose.
 - One fopen per unit on the game thread, bounded only by
   TAGPU_PK_MAX_UNITS (16384). The dump takes one open for the block.
 - The `!ta` early return skipped the s_lastFilled stash, so roster_log
   could read a packet other than the one just filled. The stamp moved to
   the top of fill_frame. Round 2 re-derived this and found it complete.
 - An unreplicated "13 361 draws/s" (the next run read 7 388) and a
   "twice on both lanes" that the recorded figures contradict (77/151).

ROUND 2's FINDINGS, ALL ACTED ON IN 76077e4.
 - MEDIUM, ta-drive: "`tacli shot` -- the engine's own surface -- does
   work" on gdi is false. NEITHER capture verb leaves the GL lane:
   tagpu_shot.trigger is polled only in render_ogl.c and
   tagpu_glshot.trigger only there and in tagpu_scaffold.c, itself a GL
   pass. Measured failing on gdi AND on Vulkan. Landing 11 deletes
   render_ogl.c and has to rewire both.
 - MEDIUM, the plan: an unchanged context line still described the force
   as asking "the shortest of the three cadences" -- it asks the longest,
   and the corrected paragraph sat twenty lines below it.
 - MEDIUM, gpu-status: "with `gap` climbing beside it" is false for gdi.
   cGap's sole increment is in tagpu_packet_acquire (tagpu_packet.c:735),
   which nothing on that lane calls, so only overrun moves there.
 - LOW: `tacli roster` labelled each block with the header from the
   PREVIOUS roster_log call -- 500 ms older than the screen= values it
   labels and from a different eye than their projection. roster_log
   emits the dump then its header, so the block's own header is the first
   one below it; the reverse scan overwrote eye on every header it passed.
   Fixed in tools/tacli and verified on a real gdi log with every header's
   eye rewritten to its own line number: old reader 1200, new 1206, block
   1201..1202, unit list identical. Pre-existing in shape (log_units had
   it too) but fixed here because this landing rewrote the line it reads.
 - LOW: s_rosDump was stamped before the fopen, so a failed open dropped a
   whole block silently, left `tacli roster` reporting "no roster lines
   yet (needs a running game)" for a running game, and put the retry 5 s
   out. Stamped by the open now, and it says once if the open fails.
 - LOW/NIT: ta-capture's surviving "300-frame roster/swept lines (10 s
   stale)" clause; two _snprintf buffers without the termination guard the
   third had; and a "97 lines"/"eleven units" pair that does not divide.
 - Plus a comment for the s_rosFill stamp, whose position ABOVE the
   in_game guard is load-bearing: below it, a persistent !in_game fill
   would freeze the clock and make the force due on every in-play draw.

WHAT RE-MEASURING DISPROVED, AFTER ROUND 2. The plan asserted that where
nothing consumes, the only fills are the forced ones, so `mouse:` would
run at 500 ms and read 1:1 against `units:`. Run A read 92/92 and agreed.
Run B (fx-lasers, ~90 s, gdi, the same build plus round 2's fixes) read
192/376 -- a clean 2:1, with `mouse, mouse, units` repeating through the
log, so fills are 250-500 ms apart. The force is a FLOOR on the fill
rate, not a ceiling: pkx_publish skips only when the cell it is about to
write is FRESH (tagpu_packet.c:315), and which cell that is depends on
the XCHG rotation rather than on whether anyone consumed. How many get
through is NOT derived, and gdi prints no counter that would settle it.
The notes now carry the range and say it is underived. It costs nothing
in correctness: `mouse:` is the line nothing reads, and the other two
hold their own clocks inside roster_log.

WHAT IS NOT COVERED. The gdi exchange counters cannot be read at all --
the `packet:` heartbeat comes from tagpu_packet_frame_end, whose only
callers are render_ogl.c and render_vk.c -- so every statement about the
gdi fill rate is from the code or from line counts, never from a counter.
The per-unit dump cost at a realistic unit count was never measured; the
one-open bound is why it does not need to be. No third review round was
run: what changed after round 2 is one moved assignment in the DLL, a
three-line reorder in tools/tacli verified by running it, two buffer
guards, comments and prose.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
RETRACTION, added 2026-09-18 while landing 11-1.

The section above headed "WHAT RE-MEASURING DISPROVED, AFTER ROUND 2"
is itself wrong, and nothing in this landing's code depends on it.

Run B was NOT on gdi. `tacli scenario load` calls write_ddraw_ini
whenever the scenario carries a setup.res -- fx-lasers carries
1024x768 -- and that function writes `renderer=openglcore`
unconditionally, so the instance's hand-set `renderer=gdi` did not
survive the load. The 192/376 figures are an OpenGL lane, where a
consumer takes every packet, fills run at ~16 ms, and `mouse:` and
`units:` therefore run at their own 250 ms and 500 ms gates -- the 2:1
that was read as evidence about gdi is the documented behaviour of a
lane with a consumer.

So the claim that "the force is a floor on the fill rate, not a
ceiling" is withdrawn. The original statement stands: where nothing
consumes, the only fills are the forced ones, one per ROSTER_HDR_MS,
and run A's gdi 92/92 is the measurement. The notes are corrected and
the harness trap is now written up in the ta-drive skill, which is
where it will be looked for: a measurement is not of the lane you set,
it is of the lane the process actually ran.
